      article.main
        h1 Plain Text
        p
          | Pug provides four ways of getting
          em plain text
          | — that is, any code or text content that should go, mostly unprocessed, directly into the rendered HTML. They are useful in different situations.
        p
          | Plain text does still use tag and string
          a(href="interpolation.html") interpolation
          | , but the first word on the line is not a Pug tag. And because plain text is not escaped, you can also include literal HTML.
        p One common pitfall here is managing whitespace in the rendered HTML. We’ll talk about that at the end of this page.
        h2#inline-in-a-tag
          | Inline in a Tag
          a.header-anchor(href="#inline-in-a-tag" aria-hidden="true") ¶
        p
          | The easiest way to add plain text is
          em inline
          | . The first term on the line is the tag itself. Everything after the tag and one space will be the text contents of that tag. This is most useful when the plain text content is short (or if you don’t mind lines running long).
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive" data-index="0")
          div.row(data-reactroot data-reactid="1" data-react-checksum="1574800426")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  p This is plain old <em>text</em> content.
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly data-reactid="7").
                  <p>This is plain old <em>text</em> content.</p>
        h2#literal-html
          | Literal HTML
          a.header-anchor(href="#literal-html" aria-hidden="true") ¶
        p
          | Whole lines are also treated as plain text when they begin with a left angle bracket (
          code <
          | ), which may occasionally be useful for writing literal HTML tags in places that could otherwise be inconvenient. For example, one use case is
          a(href="comments.html#conditional-comments") conditional comments
          | . Since literal HTML tags do not get processed, they do not self-close, unlike Pug tags.
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive" data-index="1")
          div.row(data-reactroot data-reactid="1" data-react-checksum="-1968254664")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  <html>
                  
                  body
                    p Indenting the body tag here would make no difference.
                    p HTML itself isn't whitespace-sensitive.
                  
                  </html>
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly data-reactid="7").
                  <html>
                  
                  <body>
                    <p>Indenting the body tag here would make no difference.</p>
                    <p>HTML itself isn't whitespace-sensitive.</p>
                  </body>
                  
                  </html>
        h2#piped-text
          | Piped Text
          a.header-anchor(href="#piped-text" aria-hidden="true") ¶
        p
          | Another way to add plain text to templates is to prefix a line with a pipe character (
          code |
          | ). This method is useful for mixing plain text with inline tags, as we discuss later, in the Whitespace Control section.
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive" data-index="2")
          div.row(data-reactroot data-reactid="1" data-react-checksum="1103078438")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  p
                    | The pipe always goes at the beginning of its own line,
                    | not counting indentation.
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly data-reactid="7").
                  <p>The pipe always goes at the beginning of its own line,
                    not counting indentation.</p>
        h2#block-in-a-tag
          | Block in a Tag
          a.header-anchor(href="#block-in-a-tag" aria-hidden="true") ¶
        p
          | Often you might want large blocks of text within a tag. A good example is writing JavaScript and CSS code in the
          code script
          | and
          code style
          | tags. To do this, just add a
          code .
          | right after the tag name, or after the closing parenthesis, if the tag has
          a(href="attributes.html") attributes
          | .
        p There should be no space between the tag and the dot. Plain text contents of the tag must be indented one level:
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive" data-index="3")
          div.row(data-reactroot data-reactid="1" data-react-checksum="930458921")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  script.
                    if (usingPug)
                      console.log('you are awesome')
                    else
                      console.log('use pug')
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly data-reactid="7").
                  <script>
                    if (usingPug)
                      console.log('you are awesome')
                    else
                      console.log('use pug')
                  </script>
        p
          | You can also create a dot block of plain text
          em after
          | other tags within the parent tag.
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive" data-index="4")
          div.row(data-reactroot data-reactid="1" data-react-checksum="-1476416644")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  div
                    p This text belongs to the paragraph tag.
                    br
                    .
                      This text belongs to the div tag.
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly data-reactid="7").
                  <div>
                    <p>This text belongs to the paragraph tag.</p><br />This text belongs to the div tag.
                  </div>
        h2#whitespace-control
          | Whitespace Control
          a.header-anchor(href="#whitespace-control" aria-hidden="true") ¶
        p Managing the whitespace of the rendered HTML is one of the trickiest parts about learning Pug. Don’t worry, though, you’ll get the hang of it soon enough.
        p You just need to remember two main points about how whitespace works. When compiling to HTML:
        ol
          li
            | Pug removes
            em indentation
            | , and all whitespace
            em between elements
            | .
            ul
              li
                | So, the closing tag of an HTML element will touch the opening tag of the next. This is generally not a problem for block-level elements like paragraphs, because they will still render as separate paragraphs in the web browser (unless you have changed their CSS
                code display
                | property). See the methods described below, however, for when you do need to insert space between elements.
          li
            | Pug
            em preserves
            | whitespace
            em within
            | elements, including:
            ul
              li all whitespace in the middle of a line of text.
              li leading whitespace beyond the block indentation.
              li trailing whitespace.
              li line breaks within a plain text block, or between consecutive piped lines.
        p So…Pug drops the whitespace between tags, but keeps the whitespace inside them. The value here is that it gives you full control over whether tags and/or plain text should touch. It even lets you place tags in the middle of words.
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive" data-index="5")
          div.row(data-reactroot data-reactid="1" data-react-checksum="-1715629429")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  | You put the em
                  em pha
                  | sis on the wrong syl
                  em la
                  | ble.
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly data-reactid="7").
                  You put the em<em>pha</em>sis on the wrong syl<em>la</em>ble.
        p
          | The trade-off is that it
          em requires
          | you to think about and take control over whether tags and text touch.
        p If you need the text and/or tags to touch — perhaps you need a period to appear outside the hyperlink at the end of a sentence — this is easy, as it’s basically what happens unless you tell Pug otherwise.
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive" data-index="6")
          div.row(data-reactroot data-reactid="1" data-react-checksum="1527348729")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  a ...sentence ending with a link
                  | .
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly data-reactid="7").
                  <a>...sentence ending with a link</a>.
        p
          | If you need to
          em add
          | space, you have a few options:
        h3#recommended-solutions
          | Recommended Solutions
          a.header-anchor(href="#recommended-solutions" aria-hidden="true") ¶
        p You could add one or more empty piped lines — a pipe with either spaces or nothing after it. This will insert whitespace in the rendered HTML.
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive" data-index="7")
          div.row(data-reactroot data-reactid="1" data-react-checksum="512593185")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  | Don't
                  |
                  button#self-destruct touch
                  |
                  | me!
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly data-reactid="7").
                  Don't
                  <button id="self-destruct">touch</button>
                  me!
        p
          | If your inline tags don’t require many attributes, you may find it easiest to use tag interpolation, or literal HTML, within a plain text
          em block
          | .
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive" data-index="8")
          div.row(data-reactroot data-reactid="1" data-react-checksum="-371334816")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  p.
                    Using regular tags can help keep your lines short,
                    but interpolated tags may be easier to #[em visualize]
                    whether the tags and text are whitespace-separated.
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly data-reactid="7").
                  <p>Using regular tags can help keep your lines short,
                    but interpolated tags may be easier to <em>visualize</em>
                    whether the tags and text are whitespace-separated.</p>
        h3#not-recommended
          | Not recommended
          a.header-anchor(href="#not-recommended" aria-hidden="true") ¶
        p
          | Depending on where you need the whitespace, you could add an extra space at the beginning of the text (after the block indentation, pipe character, and/or tag). Or you could add a trailing space at the
          em end
          | of the text.
        p
          strong NOTE the trailing and leading spaces here:
        div.preview-wrapper(data-control="interactive" data-index="9")
          div.row(data-reactroot data-reactid="1" data-react-checksum="-1385126330")
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="2")
              div(data-reactid="3")
                textarea(data-reactid="4").
                  | Hey, check out 
                  a(href="http://example.biz/kitteh.png") this picture
                  |  of my cat!
            div.col-lg-6(data-reactid="5")
              div(data-reactid="6")
                textarea(readonly data-reactid="7").
                  Hey, check out <a href="http://example.biz/kitteh.png">this picture</a> of my cat!
        p
          | The above solution works perfectly well, but is admittedly perhaps a little dangerous: many code editors by default will
          em remove
          | trailing whitespace on save. You and all your contributors may have to configure your editors to prevent automatic trailing whitespace removal.
        div(style="margin-top: 50px")
          div.AW-Form-692213006
